              <h2>What Delaware County Homeowners Are Actually Remodeling in 2026</h2>
              <p>Generic remodeling guides tell you kitchens and bathrooms are popular. What they don't tell you is <em>exactly how</em> Delaware County homeowners are choosing to remodel — and what the work actually involves on the ground. Based on ProBrothers' active 2025–2026 project portfolio across Media, Springfield, Swarthmore, and surrounding townships, here is where homeowners are investing:</p>

              <h3>Project Breakdown from ProBrothers' Portfolio</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li><strong>Kitchen Remodels (Open-Plan Conversions)</strong> — The most frequent request is opening the kitchen to the dining or living area by removing a structural or non-structural wall. This transforms cramped, isolated kitchens into functional family spaces. Most projects include full cabinet replacement, new flooring, and updated tile work.</li>
                <li><strong>Full Bathroom Gut Renovations</strong> — Homeowners are replacing everything: tile, toilet, vanity, sink, and shower. The goal is a clean, custom look rather than an incremental refresh. Zero-threshold showers and custom vanities are the two most common upgrades requested.</li>
                <li><strong>Full-Home Remodels (Multi-Area Scope)</strong> — A meaningful portion of ProBrothers' Delaware County projects are multi-room or whole-home in scope — combining kitchen, bathroom, basement, and exterior work into a single integrated contract. These projects are typically undertaken by homeowners who have owned their property for 10+ years and are investing for long-term residency rather than resale.</li>
                <li><strong>Basement Finishing</strong> — Converting unfinished basements into livable space — typically with a bedroom, full bathroom, and common area — is a growing priority. Delaware County homes with partially finished or unfinished basements represent significant untapped living space.</li>
                <li><strong>Living Space Conversions</strong> — Spare bedrooms are being converted into dedicated home offices, libraries, and multi-use spaces. These projects focus on built-in cabinetry, panel walls, and ceiling detail to make the room feel intentional rather than improvised.</li>
              </ul>

