            <!-- ===== NEW: PA UCC Permits and Planning ===== -->
            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Permits and Planning in 2026: What Delaware County Homeowners Need to Know</h2>
              <p>Pennsylvania's <strong>Uniform Construction Code (UCC)</strong>, enacted under <a href="https://www.legis.state.pa.us/cfdocs/legis/li/uconsCheck.cfm?txtType=HTM&yr=1999&sessInd=0&smthLwInd=0&act=45" rel="noopener" target="_blank">73 Pa. Stat. § 517.1</a>, establishes the building permit framework for all municipalities in the Commonwealth — including every township and borough in Delaware County. Understanding when a permit is required, what it costs, and how long it takes is essential to planning a realistic project timeline.</p>

              <h3>Which Projects Require a Permit in Delaware County?</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li><strong>Home Additions</strong> — Any project that adds living space — second-story additions, room expansions, attached garages, sunrooms — requires a building permit. Structural drawings stamped by a licensed engineer are typically required.</li>
                <li><strong>Structural Wall Removal</strong> — Removing or modifying a load-bearing wall requires a permit, structural calculations, and inspection before drywall closes. This applies to the open-plan kitchen conversions that are among the most popular projects in the county.</li>
                <li><strong>Basement Finishing</strong> — Converting an unfinished basement to livable space (adding walls, egress windows, a bedroom, or a bathroom) requires a permit under both the UCC and local zoning codes. Egress window sizing is regulated by the International Residential Code (IRC) as adopted by Pennsylvania.</li>
                <li><strong>Electrical, Plumbing, and HVAC Work</strong> — Any system-level work — new circuits, panel upgrades, relocating plumbing, adding HVAC zones — requires a trade permit and inspection, regardless of whether it is part of a larger remodeling project.</li>
                <li><strong>Roofing</strong> — Full roof replacements require a permit in most Delaware County municipalities; repair and patch work generally does not.</li>
                <li><strong>Window Replacement</strong> — Direct structural replacements (same opening size, no framing changes) typically do not require a permit. Enlarging a window opening requires one.</li>
                <li><strong>Deck and Porch Additions</strong> — All deck and porch structures attached to the home require a permit and must meet footing, ledger, and structural requirements.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Projects That Typically Do Not Require a Permit</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>Interior painting and wallpaper</li>
                <li>Flooring replacement (no structural changes)</li>
                <li>Cabinet replacement (no plumbing or electrical relocation)</li>
                <li>Countertop replacement</li>
                <li>Minor repairs and maintenance</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Delaware County Permit Costs and Processing Times (2026)</h3>
              <p>Permit fees and timelines vary by township and project scope. As a general guide based on Delaware County municipalities:</p>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li><strong>Permit fees</strong> typically range from <strong>$150 to $800+</strong>, calculated as a percentage of total project value or on a flat-rate schedule depending on the township.</li>
                <li><strong>Processing times</strong> range from <strong>1 to 3 weeks</strong> for most residential projects in standard townships; municipalities with active code enforcement officers may be faster. Complex additions with structural review can take 3–6 weeks.</li>
                <li><strong>Inspections</strong> are required at key stages — framing, rough electrical/plumbing, insulation, and final — and must be scheduled and passed before each subsequent stage closes.</li>
              </ul>

              <p>ProBrothers files for and manages all required permits on every project we undertake. We handle the paperwork, coordinate inspections, and ensure every phase of work is code-compliant before it closes. For homeowners planning a remodel in Delaware County, this is one of the most practical reasons to hire a local, experienced contractor rather than managing permits independently. For more on contractor vetting and permit responsibilities, see our guide to <a href="/blog/guide-to-hiring-remodeling-contractors/">hiring remodeling contractors</a> and our <a href="/blog/top-general-contractors-in-delaware-county-pa/">top general contractors in Delaware County</a> post.</p>
            </div>

